WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:03.360
Throughout Scream 2, we see a few glimpses&nbsp;
of the movie Stab! which is essentially&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:03.360 --> 00:00:06.720
the in-universe version of Scream 1.&nbsp;
Each character’s Stab! counterpart is&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:06.720 --> 00:00:09.520
played by a known actor from our&nbsp;
world, and in the case of Sidney,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:09.520 --> 00:00:13.040
that actress is Tori Spelling, which is&nbsp;
playing off of a joke from the original Scream.

00:00:13.040 --> 00:00:14.720
– [Tatum] Hey, Sid, just think. If they make a&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:14.720 --> 00:00:18.800
movie about you, who's gonna play you?
– I see you as a young Meg Ryan myself.&nbsp;

00:00:18.800 --> 00:00:21.280
– Thanks Dewey, with my luck,&nbsp;
they'd cast Tori Spelling.

00:00:21.280 --> 00:00:24.320
– It’s at one point suggested that the&nbsp;
new killer is trying to make an in&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:24.320 --> 00:00:27.200
real life sequel to the happenings&nbsp;
in Woodsboro from the year before,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:27.200 --> 00:00:30.800
and Scream 2 consequently features many&nbsp;
scenes that call back to the original. To&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:30.800 --> 00:00:34.320
learn the meaning behind the graffiti,&nbsp;
stick around to the end of this video.

00:00:34.320 --> 00:00:36.000
– And I’ll be back when you guys start talking&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:36.000 --> 00:00:38.041
about something a little&nbsp;
more Saved by the Bell ish.

00:00:38.041 --> 00:00:42.512
(metal music)

00:00:44.407 --> 00:00:50.080
– Welcome to Things You Missed. I am Zac Morris. As&nbsp;
I discussed last episode, Scream 1 was a satire on&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:50.080 --> 00:00:54.800
horror movies, so naturally Scream 2 satirizes&nbsp;
sequels. In fact, it openly acknowledges that&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:54.800 --> 00:00:58.960
they’re usually inferior to the original… so&nbsp;
what’s the deal here? Did they intentionally&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:58.960 --> 00:01:02.960
make Scream 2 an inferior movie? In the movie&nbsp;
premiere scene, Maureen gets flack from her&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:02.960 --> 00:01:08.000
fellow movie goers when she *loudly* encourages&nbsp;
the character on screen to use some common sense.

00:01:08.000 --> 00:01:12.320
Scream 2 features previously intelligent&nbsp;
characters doing things that make you want to&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:12.320 --> 00:01:16.560
*ahem* Scream at the television, like&nbsp;
splitting up with a killer in the vicinity,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:16.560 --> 00:01:21.040
backing up against a window and going back&nbsp;
to unmask someone when you should just run.

00:01:21.040 --> 00:01:23.920
– [Randy] The way I see it,&nbsp;
someone’s out to make a sequel.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:23.920 --> 00:01:26.320
You know, cash in on all the movie murder hoopla.

00:01:26.320 --> 00:01:30.240
– [Zac] …says Randy, as he enjoys his product&nbsp;
placement. Let’s get into the Things You Missed.

00:01:30.240 --> 00:01:35.920
(mysterious music)

00:01:35.920 --> 00:01:39.120
In the first Scream, I talked about how the&nbsp;
movie started by pulling a page from Alfred&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:39.120 --> 00:01:43.920
Hitchcock’s Psycho by killing off it's poster girl.&nbsp;
This time, the poster features two more big stars,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:43.920 --> 00:01:47.680
Jada Pinkett and Sarah Michelle Gellar.&nbsp;
Jada’s character Maureen is the second to&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:47.680 --> 00:01:51.440
die after her boyfriend, just like Casey,&nbsp;
and Sarah’s character Cici is third.

00:01:51.440 --> 00:01:54.880
Also, speaking of pulling a page from&nbsp;
Psycho, the opening scene of Stab adds&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:54.880 --> 00:01:58.320
a part where Casey goes into the shower.&nbsp;
The shot of the tap turning on is the same&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:58.320 --> 00:02:01.840
shot from Psycho. The rest of the scene is&nbsp;
pretty accurate to what we saw in Scream,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:01.840 --> 00:02:06.160
including the conversation, which begs the&nbsp;
question: how would the creators of Stab&nbsp;

00:02:06.160 --> 00:02:11.680
know what transpired that night unless… one&nbsp;
of the participants was actually still alive?

00:02:11.680 --> 00:02:16.000
Scream 1 used Halloween as a template, a movie&nbsp;
that everybody knows and can understand how it’s&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:16.000 --> 00:02:20.640
being satirized. But Scream 2 doesn’t really&nbsp;
offer an equivalent. However, Stab acts as&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:20.640 --> 00:02:25.200
an in-universe stand-in for Scream 1, and we’ve&nbsp;
seen something similar in the Halloween franchise.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:25.200 --> 00:02:29.680
In Halloween III, we see that the original&nbsp;
Halloween is just a movie in that universe. Halloween III&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:29.680 --> 00:02:33.760
is also a famously panned sequel, but other than&nbsp;
that, there aren’t really any other similarities.

00:02:33.760 --> 00:02:38.000
Scream had become famous and there were already&nbsp;
movies in production trying to imitate it. So&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:38.000 --> 00:02:42.000
the challenge was on for Scream 2 to try to be&nbsp;
the best imitator. While the first Scream made its&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:42.000 --> 00:02:46.400
gimmick apparent by having the killers quiz Casey&nbsp;
on scary movies while being in a scary movie,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:46.400 --> 00:02:50.160
Scream 2 tells us that these movie fans are&nbsp;
about to be in a movie of their own&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:50.160 --> 00:02:54.080
by using screen direction. ‘Stab Casey’&nbsp;
is seen running to the left towards a door,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:54.080 --> 00:02:56.480
and then we cut to Phil going&nbsp;
left through a door himself.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:56.480 --> 00:02:59.840
This link is the director’s way of telling&nbsp;
us that he’s about to be a victim too.

00:02:59.840 --> 00:03:03.200
We hear the song Red Right Hand by&nbsp;
Nick Cave and the Bad Seeds twice&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:03.200 --> 00:03:06.880
in this movie. In the first one it played&nbsp;
during the curfew lockup montage, and in 2,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:06.880 --> 00:03:10.960
we hear it right as Stab is beginning and&nbsp;
as the Delta Lambda Zeta party disperses,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:10.960 --> 00:03:14.320
perhaps another link to tell us that&nbsp;
the movie is leaking into reality.

00:03:14.320 --> 00:03:18.960
There are a lot of instances where things in&nbsp;
Scream 2 fill the role for material from the first&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:18.960 --> 00:03:23.520
one. For example, Sidney’s college mascot is the&nbsp;
Wildcats, a less specific version of the Panthers,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:23.520 --> 00:03:27.360
which was her high school mascot. It plays&nbsp;
into the idea that we have copycat killers,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:27.360 --> 00:03:31.280
just like how the initial 3 victims share a&nbsp;
name with the first three victims in Woodsboro.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:31.280 --> 00:03:35.120
Sidney once again gets unwanted attention from the&nbsp;
press because of her past. The scene where Cici&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:35.120 --> 00:03:39.360
is attacked at her sorority home is just like the&nbsp;
scene of Sidney being attacked at her actual home.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:39.360 --> 00:03:43.280
And just like Scream 1, Ghostface pops out&nbsp;
from a closet by the stairs. I wonder&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:43.280 --> 00:03:46.960
if the fact that he hides “under the stairs”&nbsp;
is a Wes Craven reference. Cici’s immediate&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:46.960 --> 00:03:50.560
reaction is to then run up the stairs, just&nbsp;
like high school Sidney said she would.

00:03:50.560 --> 00:03:53.760
– Some stupid killer stalking some&nbsp;
big breasted girl who can’t act&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:53.760 --> 00:03:56.160
who’s always running up the stairs when&nbsp;
she should be going out the front door.

00:03:56.160 --> 00:03:59.280
– The ice cream scene is the new video&nbsp;
store scene. In the video store scene,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:59.280 --> 00:04:03.840
Randy directly determines who one of the killers&nbsp;
is by accusing Billy, who fires back by asking…

00:04:03.840 --> 00:04:05.360
– How do we know you’re not the killer?

00:04:05.360 --> 00:04:08.160
– And Randy admits that if this were&nbsp;
a scary movie, he would, in fact,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:08.160 --> 00:04:12.080
be a prime suspect. This time, he correctly&nbsp;
identifies Mickey as one of the killers.

00:04:12.080 --> 00:04:14.320
– The freaky Tarantino film student.

00:04:14.320 --> 00:04:17.680
– But also concedes that he would likely&nbsp;
be a suspect on the same grounds.

00:04:17.680 --> 00:04:19.600
– [Randy] But if he’s a suspect, so am I.

00:04:19.600 --> 00:04:22.160
– Worth noting in this scene, he’s&nbsp;
also kind of on the right track&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:22.160 --> 00:04:25.280
when it comes to the second killer. He&nbsp;
brings up Mrs. Voorhees as proof that&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:25.280 --> 00:04:28.560
the killer could be a female. Like&nbsp;
Mrs. Voorhees from Friday the 13th,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:28.560 --> 00:04:32.240
the killer is the mom of another killer.&nbsp;
Randy’s other suspect is Gale Weathers.

00:04:32.240 --> 00:04:34.960
– That’s what reporters do,&nbsp;
Dewey. They stage the news.

00:04:34.960 --> 00:04:38.960
– He’s got the right idea, but he’s got the wrong&nbsp;
reporter. History repeats itself later in the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:38.960 --> 00:04:44.000
movie when Gale and Dewey team up, and Gale makes&nbsp;
an important discovery while they're hooking up. In the original it’s Neil&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:44.000 --> 00:04:48.640
Prescott’s car, in the sequel it’s Mickey’s&nbsp;
tapes. The killer is once again behind Randy.

00:04:48.640 --> 00:04:49.680
– Look behind you.

00:04:49.680 --> 00:04:51.760
– Dewey gets stabbed in the back again. Gale blocks&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:51.760 --> 00:04:54.960
the door to the recording studio so that&nbsp;
Ghostface can only reach in and flail.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:54.960 --> 00:04:59.120
It’s like Sidney in her room. Mickey reveals&nbsp;
himself as the killer using a familiar line.

00:04:59.120 --> 00:05:02.080
– Surprise Sidney!

00:05:02.080 --> 00:05:04.720
– …just like Stu did during the&nbsp;
last 20 minutes of Scream.

00:05:04.720 --> 00:05:06.720
– Surprise Sidney!

00:05:06.720 --> 00:05:09.520
– At the end, Sidney buries one into&nbsp;
Mrs. Loomis’s forehead just like&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:09.520 --> 00:05:13.520
she had to her son, and it all ends with&nbsp;
Cotton foreshadowing the next Stab film.

00:05:13.520 --> 00:05:16.720
– [Cotton] I’ll tell you one thing.&nbsp;
It will make a hell of a movie.

00:05:16.720 --> 00:05:19.120
– But there is one easter egg that even I wouldn’t&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:19.120 --> 00:05:22.291
have found if not for this&nbsp;
retrospective documentary.

00:05:22.291 --> 00:05:26.267
(cinematic impact)

00:05:27.314 --> 00:05:31.284
(dark music)

00:05:31.284 --> 00:05:34.800
I’m sure you noticed the way Ghostface&nbsp;
wipes off the knife in Scream 2. It’s&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:34.800 --> 00:05:38.320
a signature move established by Stu in the&nbsp;
first movie. I wanted to bring it up though,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:38.320 --> 00:05:41.920
because in my video on the first film,&nbsp;
I tried to, as I do, figure out what&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:41.920 --> 00:05:46.480
the subtext might be behind this. However,&nbsp;
in the 2011 retrospective Still Screaming,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:46.480 --> 00:05:50.320
Dane Farwell, the stunt actor in that&nbsp;
scene offers a simpler explanation.

00:05:50.320 --> 00:05:53.920
– It’s a direct cut to me running&nbsp;
through the hall with a clean knife.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:53.920 --> 00:05:57.440
Wes just thought for two seconds and said,&nbsp;
“We’ll wipe the knife off on camera.”

00:05:57.440 --> 00:06:00.640
– Another thing I talked about in my first&nbsp;
video was relevant cameo appearances,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:00.640 --> 00:06:04.000
and those seem to have been given&nbsp;
special attention here in 1997.

00:06:04.000 --> 00:06:08.160
Director of Photography Peter Deming plays the&nbsp;
‘Popcorn Boy’. Screenwriter Kevin Williamson&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:08.160 --> 00:06:11.520
is the interviewer on “Current Edition”.&nbsp;
I never would have noticed this on my own,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:11.520 --> 00:06:14.000
but Matthew Lillard, who plays Stu in Scream,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:14.000 --> 00:06:18.480
used 2% of his power to make a background cameo&nbsp;
at the party scene, which thankfully for me,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:18.480 --> 00:06:22.080
he brought up in the retrospective, otherwise&nbsp;
I never would have known it was him.

00:06:22.080 --> 00:06:25.680
– One day I was just hanging out and they were&nbsp;
like ‘come walk in the back of the party scene’.

00:06:25.680 --> 00:06:27.920
– And director Wes Craven makes another appearance,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:27.920 --> 00:06:31.840
this time as a doctor at the hospital scene. That&nbsp;
one I actually found out about from a fan site,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:31.840 --> 00:06:36.160
which is coincidentally also called “Still&nbsp;
Screaming.” The best cameo though is this one,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:36.160 --> 00:06:41.200
from Lev Shrieber’s dog. What a boy. There are a&nbsp;
bunch of other cameos, but on Things You Missed,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:41.200 --> 00:06:45.040
I’m only interested in ones that have some kind&nbsp;
of significance or something to do with the movie.

00:06:45.040 --> 00:06:48.560
After a film class discussion on&nbsp;
sequels and imitators of movie killers,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:48.560 --> 00:06:51.520
Gale brushes off the idea of&nbsp;
audience safety being important.

00:06:52.603 --> 00:06:55.200
– Are you kidding me? It’d&nbsp;
be stupid to pull this movie.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:55.840 --> 00:06:58.480
All this free press they’re gonna&nbsp;
have huge numbers this weekend.

00:06:58.480 --> 00:07:02.720
–  [Zac] You either die a hero, or live long&nbsp;
enough to see yourself become the villain.

00:07:02.720 --> 00:07:05.600
Despite this movie’s concept:&nbsp;
being a take-off of horror sequels,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:05.600 --> 00:07:09.440
we aren’t bombarded with movie references like&nbsp;
we were in Scream. Instead, there’s another&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:09.440 --> 00:07:13.360
theme that the finale centers itself around:&nbsp;
theatre. Sidney is a theatre major and has a&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:13.360 --> 00:07:17.280
prominent role in the production of Agamemnon,&nbsp;
which is advertised all over the campus.

00:07:18.423 --> 00:07:22.800
– [Gus] Cassandra is one of the great tragic&nbsp;
visionaries of literature. She saw it all coming.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:22.800 --> 00:07:29.120
The wars, the murder, the madness. She knew she&nbsp;
was cursed. It was her fate and she embraced it.

00:07:29.120 --> 00:07:33.680
– This might be a comparison between two unlikely&nbsp;
subjects to be paired, horror movie final girls&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:33.680 --> 00:07:37.280
and greek mythology. One of the things that&nbsp;
audiences tend to find ridiculous about&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:37.280 --> 00:07:40.880
sequels is the fact that the same characters&nbsp;
get themselves into trouble again. It always&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:40.880 --> 00:07:45.455
seems like a huge coincidence that only exists to&nbsp;
extend the franchise. That’s why in a lot of long&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:45.455 --> 00:07:49.680
running franchises, there’s some kind of curse&nbsp;
that keeps the killer coming back again and again.

00:07:49.680 --> 00:07:53.360
The character Sidney plays in Agamemnon is&nbsp;
Cassandra, who is literally cursed by the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:53.360 --> 00:07:56.880
Greek God Apollo. In the case of Scream,&nbsp;
it’s obviously not like a magic curse,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:56.880 --> 00:08:00.560
but rather that Sidney is cursed by the publicity&nbsp;
of the horrible things that have happened to her.

00:08:00.560 --> 00:08:03.680
I do think it’s significant that their&nbsp;
whole culture on campus revolves around&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:03.680 --> 00:08:07.920
these sororities, or as they are sometimes called,&nbsp;
Greek Life. In the car crash scene, there’s some&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:07.920 --> 00:08:11.520
graffiti of the Greek letters representing the&nbsp;
sorority where the party took place earlier, Delta&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:11.520 --> 00:08:16.000
Lambda Zeta, along with text in English that says&nbsp;
“stab this”. But the Greek letters are crossed&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:16.000 --> 00:08:21.040
out in red, and this is the scene where their&nbsp;
pledge, Hallie, gets herself crossed off by Mickey.

00:08:21.040 --> 00:08:24.400
The movie’s climax takes place on the&nbsp;
stage where the “Fall of Troy” set is still&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:24.400 --> 00:08:27.360
up. This is where Ghostface attacks&nbsp;
her on more than one occasion. The&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:27.360 --> 00:08:31.600
Greek vs. the Trojan so to speak. At the&nbsp;
end she actually uses her Greek letters,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:31.600 --> 00:08:35.440
given to her by her boyfriend to protect her,&nbsp;
to attack Mickey, and ultimately get away.

00:08:35.440 --> 00:08:39.360
The play that she’s in is Agamemnon,&nbsp;
which like Stab, is the first chapter of a&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:39.360 --> 00:08:43.275
trilogy. Scream&nbsp;
was a trilogy from 2000-2011. 

00:08:43.388 --> 00:08:44.487
(rock music)

00:08:44.487 --> 00:08:47.840
– [Mickey] Empire Strikes Back. It was a&nbsp;
better story. Improved effects.&nbsp;

00:08:47.840 --> 00:08:50.480
– [Randy] Not a sequel, part of&nbsp;
a trilogy, completely planned.

00:08:50.480 --> 00:08:53.920
– In Scream, I think I pointed out like&nbsp;
20 different Nightmare on Elm Street references,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:53.920 --> 00:08:58.560
but Scream 2 seems to just have one, when Cotton&nbsp;
is arrested by Sidney’s bodyguards in the library&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:58.560 --> 00:09:02.480
we see a book called Our Dreaming Mind, which&nbsp;
deals with the science of sleep and dreams,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:02.480 --> 00:09:05.892
much like how Nancy visits a sleep disorder&nbsp;
clinic to try fix her Freddy Fr--

00:09:05.892 --> 00:09:06.392
(BEEP)

00:09:06.392 --> 00:09:08.654
(laughing) to try to fix her Freddy Freeman problem...

00:09:08.654 --> 00:09:09.154
(BEEP)

00:09:09.154 --> 00:09:11.128
...to try to fix her Freddy Krueger problem.

00:09:11.128 --> 00:09:14.320
For a minute, I thought this was Freddy’s&nbsp;
iconic sweater in Sid’s dorm room,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:14.320 --> 00:09:17.360
but when I actually compared them, I&nbsp;
realized they’re not the same. I had&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:17.360 --> 00:09:20.000
the same thing happen in Sid’s room&nbsp;
when I analyzed the first Scream,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:20.000 --> 00:09:23.520
I thought this was Chucky’s sweater, but&nbsp;
eventually realized the colors don’t match.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:23.520 --> 00:09:27.360
Scream 2 has a girl that looks kind of like&nbsp;
Chucky, but again, the colors don’t match.

00:09:27.360 --> 00:09:30.000
As I did for the first movie, I want&nbsp;
to wrap up by talking about some of&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:30.000 --> 00:09:33.601
the evidence about who the killers are,&nbsp;
some of which you might have missed.

00:09:33.696 --> 00:09:37.636
(cinematic impact)

00:09:38.630 --> 00:09:40.630
(spooky dance music)

00:09:42.560 --> 00:09:47.120
First up we’ve got Cici’s sorority house, where we&nbsp;
can confirm that there are once again, two killers&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:47.120 --> 00:09:50.960
because you can still hear one Ghostface talking&nbsp;
on the phone as the other sneaks through the front&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:50.960 --> 00:09:55.771
door in the background. During certain chase scenes, we&nbsp;
hear Ghostface grunting. Sometimes it sounds male…

00:09:55.771 --> 00:09:57.615
(male grunting sounds)

00:09:57.615 --> 00:09:59.517
– … and at other times, the sound is female.

00:09:59.517 --> 00:10:01.013
(female grunting sounds)

00:10:01.013 --> 00:10:04.640
– But when speaking on the phone, we can’t make this&nbsp;
determination because of the voice changer. When&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:04.640 --> 00:10:09.920
Sidney gets a Ghostface call from Debra Salt aka&nbsp;
Mrs. Loomis at the sorority house, she hears this…

00:10:11.048 --> 00:10:14.560
– [Caller] Hello Sidney. Remember me?

00:10:14.560 --> 00:10:18.720
– …suggesting that it’s someone from Sidney’s&nbsp;
past, like her late ex-boyfriend’s mom.

00:10:18.720 --> 00:10:22.320
And from a filmmaking standpoint, when you make a&nbsp;
sequel, you have to include enough information so&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:22.320 --> 00:10:26.480
that even audiences who haven’t seen the first one&nbsp;
can keep up. We only see two scenes from Stab,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:26.480 --> 00:10:30.560
the opening and this scene, which reminds&nbsp;
us that Billy’s Mom left him and his Dad.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:30.560 --> 00:10:34.480
They only reason to cut away from the scene we’re&nbsp;
in and show that part is if that information&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:34.480 --> 00:10:39.200
is going to somehow be important. Like if&nbsp;
Billy’s Mom were to, for example, come back.

00:10:39.200 --> 00:10:42.240
Debra also suspiciously manages&nbsp;
to get to the scene of the crime&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:42.240 --> 00:10:45.120
at the sorority house where Cici was&nbsp;
attacked and at the film school where&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:45.120 --> 00:10:48.800
Dewey was attacked before anyone else.&nbsp;
Maybe because she was already there.

00:10:48.800 --> 00:10:52.560
But the strongest evidence is actually the&nbsp;
evidence for Mickey being guilty. In class,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:52.560 --> 00:10:55.520
he argues for the idea that sequels can&nbsp;
be better than the original. Of course&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:55.520 --> 00:10:58.880
he would say that. The Windsor murders&nbsp;
are his sequel to the Woodsboro murders.

00:10:58.880 --> 00:11:02.400
– [Sidney] You know, I knew&nbsp;
this was coming? I knew&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:02.400 --> 00:11:05.120
this wasn’t over?
– [Mickey] You are not alone Sid.

00:11:05.120 --> 00:11:08.080
– Yeah, he would know that this was coming&nbsp;
because he was responsible. The most&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:08.080 --> 00:11:11.680
damning evidence though is the fact that he&nbsp;
always has his video camera. When Dewey and&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:11.680 --> 00:11:14.720
Gale go to study their tapes, they see&nbsp;
each of the crime scenes was recorded&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:14.720 --> 00:11:18.240
from Ghostface’s point of view. The only&nbsp;
issue is that he isn’t the one projecting&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:18.240 --> 00:11:21.840
these tapes. He must have given them to Mrs.&nbsp;
Loomis at some point, because he was planning&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:21.840 --> 00:11:25.280
on getting caught so he could use the trial&nbsp;
to try to blame the movies for his actions.

00:11:25.280 --> 00:11:28.240
OK, I take back what I said. The&nbsp;
most damning evidence against Mickey&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:28.240 --> 00:11:31.520
is the fact that we can see him right here&nbsp;
through the eyeholes of the Ghostface mask.

00:11:31.520 --> 00:11:35.280
Scream 2 was famously rewritten during production&nbsp;
because the ending leaked on the internet,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:35.280 --> 00:11:39.120
so the fact that we have all these setups&nbsp;
is kind of impressive. We’re mostly all in&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:39.120 --> 00:11:43.120
agreement that Scream 1 is superior, and I&nbsp;
think the very idea that the sequel’s villain&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:43.120 --> 00:11:47.749
could just be switched like that is kind of a&nbsp;
testament to it being a weaker story. However,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:47.749 --> 00:11:51.920
with all of that being said, I guess Cotton was going&nbsp;
to be one of the villains originally? And I’m&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:51.920 --> 00:11:55.760
sort of glad that didn’t happen because his&nbsp;
redemption at the end is one of the best parts,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:55.760 --> 00:12:00.080
and it sets us up for Scream 3. Which I’ll&nbsp;
be covering next time on Things You Missed.

